From e9a79b4fff0a6b0774bc885ccc3adc446111ba19 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Vortiago <atle@havso.net>
Date: Mon, 5 Oct 2026 16:35:18 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] perf(ggml-cpu): split concat over dst rows and tile a
 transposed src1
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

The CPU concat kernels split the work over dst ne2 only. In the conv-state
concat of the delta-net and mamba models, ggml_concat(state,
ggml_transpose(x), 0), ne2 is the number of sequences. It is 1 in
single-sequence prefill and in decode, so one thread copies the whole
[3 + n_tokens, channels] tensor element by element and the other threads
wait at the barrier.

- Replace concat_i8, concat_f16 and concat_f32 with one template on the
  element size. It splits the dst rows (ne1*ne2*ne3) with
  get_thread_range() and copies a row with memcpy when its source is
  contiguous and with a strided loop when it is not.
- For dim 0 with a transposed src1 (nb10 > nb11), copy blocks of one cache
  line of dst rows column by column. One src1 cache line then loads once,
  not once per row it holds. A column stride that is a multiple of 4096
  (40960 bytes for 10240 f32 channels) maps a whole src1 row to one L1 set,
  so a row-by-row copy reads each line from L3 up to 16 times.
- Split concat_any over dst rows too. It keeps its memcpy per row.

The op stays a pure copy, so the output is bit-identical to the old
kernels for every thread count.

test-backend-ops: add view bit 16 (b is a transposed contiguous tensor)
for F32, F16, BF16 and I8, and conv-state cases at 10240 channels for
prefill, decode and 4 sequences, plus two perf cases.

The contiguous fast path is similar in idea to ik_llama.cpp
ggml/src/ggml.c (MIT). No code is copied.

